(() => {
 const F=FRTB,U=FRTB_UI,{slider,bars,m,n,pct}=U,L=FRTB_LESSONS;
 const select=(id,label,options,value)=>({id,label,options,value});U.select=select;
 const classes=[
 {id:'girr',name:'GIRR',title:'Interest rates: a curve is more than one rate',buckets:['GBP','EUR'],rw:[.011/Math.SQRT2,.011/Math.SQRT2],curvRW:[.017/Math.SQRT2,.017/Math.SQRT2],rho:[.999,.999],gamma:.5,vegaRW:1,labels:['GBP curve A','GBP curve B','EUR curve A','EUR curve B'],profile:'Delta: two distinct 5-year yield curves in each of GBP and EUR. Curvature shifts each whole curve in parallel. Vega: 1- and 5-year option expiries on the same 5-year underlying residual tenor in each currency.',intuition:'An interest-rate hedge can match one point on a yield curve and still miss another. FRTB keeps currencies, maturities and curves visible before allowing prescribed diversification.',mapping:'Ten delta vertices run from 0.25 to 30 years. Currency defines the bucket. Inflation and cross-currency basis add separate delta factors. Vega has both option-expiry and underlying-residual-maturity dimensions.',specific:'Same maturity on different curves has 99.9% delta correlation. Different tenors on the same curve use max(exp(−0.03 × tenor gap / shorter tenor), 40%); different curves and tenors multiply this by 99.9%. Yield–inflation correlation is 40%; cross-currency basis has zero correlation with other factors. Cross-currency bucket correlation is 50%.',pages:[[323,'325l · GIRR factors'],[343,'325ae–ag · weights and correlations'],[359,'325ax–ay · vega and curvature']],rows:[...[[.25,1.7],[.5,1.7],[1,1.6],[2,1.3],[3,1.2],[5,1.1],[10,1.1],[15,1.1],[20,1.1],[30,1.1]].map(([t,w])=>[t+' years',w+'%',(w/Math.SQRT2).toFixed(4)+'%']),['Inflation / cross-currency basis','1.6%',(1.6/Math.SQRT2).toFixed(4)+'%']],refHeaders:['Delta factor','Base RW','Liquid / domestic currency RW']},
 {id:'csr',name:'CSR · non-securitisation',title:'Credit spreads: names, sectors and ratings',buckets:['3 · IG financial','5 · IG consumer'],rw:[.05,.03],rho:[.35,.35],gamma:.15,vegaRW:1,labels:['Bank A','Bank B','Consumer A','Consumer B'],profile:'Two different issuers in each of IG financial bucket 3 and IG consumer/transport bucket 5. Delta uses the same 5-year tenor and bond basis. Vega uses different issuers at the same option expiry; curvature shocks each issuer’s whole spread curve.',intuition:'A bank bond and a retailer bond can both lose value when credit conditions worsen. Their spread sensitivities do not cancel as reliably as two positions referencing the same issuer and curve.',mapping:'Delta has issuer × tenor × bond/CDS basis. The five tenors are 0.5, 1, 3, 5 and 10 years. Eighteen buckets distinguish rating quality, sector and eligible indices. Default is charged separately in DRC.',specific:'Within a bucket, multiply name, tenor and basis correlations: different names use 35% in buckets 1–15, different tenors 65%, and different bond/CDS basis 99.9%. Index buckets 17–18 use 80% for different indices. Cross-bucket correlations multiply rating and sector components; this example uses same rating × 15% sector correlation. Other bucket 16 has special absolute-sum aggregation.',pages:[[325,'325m · CSR factors'],[345,'325ah–aj · CSR weights and correlations']],rows:[['1 · Sovereign / central bank',.5],['2 · Regional / public',1],['3 · Financial',5],['4 · Basic / energy / industrial',3],['5 · Consumer / transport',3],['6 · Technology / telecom',2],['7 · Health / utilities / professional',1.5],['8 · Covered bonds',1.5],['9 · HY sovereign',2],['10 · HY regional / public',4],['11 · HY financial',12],['12 · HY basic / energy / industrial',7],['13 · HY consumer / transport',8.5],['14 · HY technology / telecom',5.5],['15 · HY health / utilities / professional',5],['16 · Other',12],['17 · IG indices',1.5],['18 · HY indices',5]].map(([a,b])=>[a,b+'%'])},
 {id:'nonactp',name:'CSR · securitisation',title:'Securitisations: watch the tranche',buckets:['1 · Prime RMBS','7 · Auto ABS'],rw:[.009,.012],rho:[.4,.4],gamma:0,vegaRW:1,labels:['RMBS tranche A','RMBS tranche B','Auto tranche A','Auto tranche B'],profile:'Senior investment-grade tranches in buckets 1 (prime RMBS) and 7 (auto ABS). Each bucket has two distinct tranches, the same 5-year tenor and basis. Vega uses the same expiry. These are outside the alternative correlation trading portfolio (non-ACTP).',intuition:'A securitisation redistributes a pool’s losses between tranches. Two tranches can reference similar loans yet have very different exposure to a worsening credit environment.',mapping:'Non-ACTP sensitivities follow the tranche spread, not individual underlying issuers. Twenty-four buckets combine eight asset classes with seniority and credit quality; bucket 25 holds other exposures.',specific:'Within-bucket delta correlation is tranche × tenor × basis: 40% for different tranches, 80% for different tenors, 99.9% for different bases. Tranches are the same for correlation only when more than 80% of notional overlaps. Cross-bucket correlation is zero. Bucket 25 is added outside the square root, with no diversification or hedging benefit.',pages:[[326,'325n · tranche risk factors'],[351,'325am–ao · non-ACTP weights and correlations']],rows:(()=>{const names=['Prime RMBS','Mid-prime RMBS','Sub-prime RMBS','CMBS','Student ABS','Credit-card ABS','Auto ABS','CLO'],w=[[.9,1.5,2,2,.8,1.2,1.2,1.4],[1.125,1.875,2.5,2.5,1,1.5,1.5,1.75],[1.575,2.625,3.5,3.5,1.4,2.1,2.1,2.45]];return w.flatMap((a,g)=>a.map((v,i)=>[(g*8+i+1)+' · '+names[i],['Senior IG','Non-senior IG','HY / unrated'][g],v+'%'])).concat([['25 · Other','Other','3.5%']]);})(),refHeaders:['Bucket / asset class','Quality / seniority','Delta RW']},
 {id:'actp',name:'CSR · correlation trading',title:'Correlation trading: look through to issuers',buckets:['3 · IG financial','5 · IG consumer'],rw:[.08,.04],rho:[.35,.35],gamma:.15,vegaRW:1,labels:['Underlying bank A','Underlying bank B','Underlying consumer A','Underlying consumer B'],profile:'ACTP credit-spread sensitivities to two underlying issuers in each of buckets 3 and 5, with common 5-year tenor and bond basis. Vega uses the same expiry. This is a sensitivity illustration, not a synthetic-CDO valuation model.',intuition:'For correlation trading, the way defaults and spreads move together matters to the tranche payoff. The risk map therefore looks through to the issuers in the underlying pool.',mapping:'ACTP means alternative correlation trading portfolio. Its eligibility is defined in Article 325 of the general provisions. Delta and vega use underlying issuers; curvature shocks their spread curves. This differs from non-ACTP tranche-spread mapping.',specific:'ACTP uses the first sixteen CSR sector buckets with its own weights. Correlations follow non-securitisation CSR, except the different-basis factor is 99% rather than 99.9%. Other bucket 16 has special aggregation. ACTP also has a separate DRC treatment and RRAO considerations.',pages:[[325,'325n · ACTP risk factors'],[349,'325ak–al · ACTP weights']],rows:[4,4,8,5,4,3,2,6,13,13,16,10,12,12,12,13].map((w,i)=>['Bucket '+(i+1),w+'%'])},
 {id:'equity',name:'Equity',title:'Equities: a market hedge is not a name hedge',buckets:['5 · Large advanced','6 · Large advanced'],rw:[.30,.35],rho:[.25,.25],gamma:.15,vegaRW:.7778,labels:['Consumer A','Consumer B','Industrial A','Industrial B'],profile:'Two distinct large-cap advanced-economy equities in each of buckets 5 (consumer/health/utilities) and 6 (telecom/industrial). Delta and curvature use spot risk; vega uses a common option expiry. Repo risk is explained separately below.',intuition:'A broad equity hedge can soften a market fall without removing company-specific moves. FRTB recognises that distinction through name and sector buckets.',mapping:'Delta distinguishes stock spot and repo curves. Vega is implied spot volatility across option expiries. Curvature is spot-only: repo has neither a vega nor a curvature charge. PRA large capitalisation means more than £1.60 billion.',specific:'Different names correlate at 15% in buckets 1–4, 25% in 5–8, 7.5% in 9, 12.5% in 10 and 80% in index buckets 12–13. Same-name spot/repo correlation is 99.9%. Cross-bucket correlation is 15% among 1–10; 75% between the two index buckets; 45% between index and 1–10; zero for bucket 11. Bucket 11 uses special absolute-sum delta aggregation.',pages:[[326,'325o · equity factors'],[354,'325ap–ar · equity weights and correlations'],[251,'325bd(9) · large capitalisation']],rows:[55,60,45,55,30,35,40,50,70,50,70,15,25].map((w,i)=>['Bucket '+(i+1),w+'%',(w/100)+'%']),refHeaders:['Bucket','Spot delta RW','Repo delta RW']},
 {id:'commodity',name:'Commodity',title:'Commodities: similar is not identical',buckets:['2 · Liquid fuels','7 · Precious metals'],rw:[.35,.20],rho:[.95,.55],gamma:.2,vegaRW:1,labels:['Brent','WTI','Gold','Silver'],profile:'Brent and WTI in liquid-fuel bucket 2; gold and silver in precious-metal bucket 7. Delta uses a common tenor and matching delivery location within each comparison. Vega uses a common expiry. Curvature shocks each commodity curve in parallel.',intuition:'Brent and WTI share an oil story, yet local supply and delivery conditions can pull them apart. A hedge with a related commodity retains basis risk.',mapping:'Delta keeps commodity, maturity and delivery location. Vega keeps commodity and option expiry. Curvature groups all maturities of a commodity and does not distinguish delivery location.',specific:'Different-commodity correlations depend on the bucket. Different tenor multiplies by 99%; different delivery location by 99.9%. The example uses 95% within liquid fuels and 55% within precious metals. Cross-bucket correlation is 20% for buckets 1–10 and zero when bucket 11 is involved. Electricity delivery regions and freight routes require careful factor mapping.',pages:[[327,'325p · commodity factors'],[357,'325as–au · commodity weights and correlations']],rows:[['1 · Solid fuels',30,55],['2 · Liquid fuels',35,95],['3a · Electricity',60,40],['3b · Carbon',60,40],['4 · Freight',80,80],['5 · Non-precious metals',40,60],['6 · Gas',45,65],['7 · Precious metals',20,55],['8 · Grains / oilseeds',35,45],['9 · Livestock',25,15],['10 · Softs / agriculture',35,40],['11 · Other',50,15]].map(([a,b,c])=>[a,b+'%',c+'%']),refHeaders:['Bucket','Delta RW','Different commodity ρ']},
 {id:'fx',name:'Foreign exchange',title:'FX: your reporting currency matters',buckets:['USD / GBP','EUR / GBP'],rw:[.15/Math.SQRT2,.15/Math.SQRT2],rho:[1,1],gamma:.6,vegaRW:1,labels:['USD / GBP','USD / GBP','EUR / GBP','EUR / GBP'],profile:'GBP reporting currency, with USD/GBP and EUR/GBP buckets. Each delta/curvature bucket has a single net factor: the two input positions in that currency pair are netted first. Vega instead uses 1- and 5-year expiries per pair.',intuition:'An asset can be unchanged in local currency while its value changes in pounds. FX capital depends on the currency in which the institution measures its results.',mapping:'Delta and curvature use exchange rates against the reporting currency (or a permitted base currency). Vega uses all applicable currency pairs and five option-expiry vertices. Onshore and offshore currency variants need not be separated.',specific:'Delta RW is 15%, divided by √2 for the specified most-liquid currency pairs. USD/GBP and EUR/GBP qualify. Cross-bucket correlation is 60%. Vega RW is 100%. The conditional 1.5 curvature divisor and permitted base-currency arrangements in Article 325q are not applied in this example.',pages:[[327,'325q · FX factors'],[359,'325av–ay · FX weights and correlations']],rows:[['Ordinary FX pair','15%'],['Eligible most-liquid pair',(15/Math.SQRT2).toFixed(4)+'%'],['Vega','100%'],['Cross-bucket delta / vega correlation','60%']]}
 ];
 function calc(c,s,sc){const mode=s.mode,inputs=[s.a,s.b,s.c,s.d],expiry=Math.exp(-.01*4),rho=c.rho.map(r=>mode==='vega'?(c.id==='girr'?expiry:c.id==='fx'?expiry:r):mode==='curvature'?r*r:r).map(r=>F.scenario(r,sc)),gamma=F.scenario(mode==='curvature'?c.gamma*c.gamma:c.gamma,sc),buckets=[],rows=[];
 for(let b=0;b<2;b++){let vals=inputs.slice(b*2,b*2+2),labels=c.labels.slice(b*2,b*2+2);if(c.id==='fx'&&mode!=='vega'){vals=[F.sum(vals)];labels=[c.buckets[b]+' · net'];}const rw=mode==='vega'?c.vegaRW:mode==='curvature'?(c.curvRW||c.rw)[b]:c.rw[b];
 if(mode==='curvature'){// Synthetic full-revaluation P&Ls; their nonlinear remainders are controlled explicitly.
 const up=vals.map(v=>v*s.up/100),down=vals.map(v=>v*s.down/100);vals.forEach((v,j)=>rows.push([labels[j],m(v),pct(rw),m(rw*v-up[j]),m(-rw*v-down[j]),m(up[j]),m(down[j])]));buckets.push({...F.curvatureBucket(up,down,rho[b]),up,down});}
 else {const sens=mode==='vega'?vals.map(v=>v*s.vol/100):vals,ws=sens.map(v=>v*rw);sens.forEach((v,j)=>rows.push([mode==='vega'&&['girr','fx'].includes(c.id)?c.buckets[b]+' · '+(j?5:1)+'y expiry':labels[j],m(v),pct(rw),m(ws[j])]));buckets.push({ws,k:F.linearBucket(ws,rho[b])});}}
 const agg=mode==='curvature'?{capital:F.curvatureAggregate(buckets,gamma),fallback:false}:F.linearAggregate(buckets,gamma);return {...agg,buckets,rows,rho,gamma};}
 window.FRTB_CLASSES=classes;window.FRTB_SBM=calc;
 L.push({id:'sbm',name:'SBM · the aggregation engine',stage:'02 · Standardised approach',title:'One portfolio, three correlation scenarios',intro:'SBM combines seven risk classes, each with delta, vega and curvature. The order of aggregation matters.',chart:'Take the largest total scenario',inputs:[slider('hedge','Opposing sensitivity',-100,100,5,-60,'%'),slider('scale','Portfolio size',0,200,5,100,'%'),slider('option','Option-risk size',0,200,5,100,'%')],challenge:'Flip the opposing sensitivity from negative to positive. Does high correlation still help?',formula:'SBM = max over low, medium, high { Σ risk classes (Delta + Vega + Curvature) }',key:'High: min(1, 1.25ρ). Low: max(2ρ − 1, 0.75ρ). Apply to both within- and cross-bucket correlations. For curvature, start from squared delta correlations.',simpleTitle:'A hedge is only as good as the relationship it relies on',simple:'Higher correlation can help opposing exposures and hurt exposures pointing in the same direction. FRTB therefore tests three possible correlation environments instead of assuming that one is always conservative.',scope:'Seven synthetic class portfolios use the named profiles in the next lessons. Delta, vega and curvature inputs are illustrative and need not correspond to one real trade set. No diversification is applied between risk classes or risk types.',pages:[[315,'325f · delta and vega aggregation'],[316,'325g · curvature aggregation'],[318,'325h · three scenarios']],notes:[['Read the bucket calculation','First net identical factors, then multiply by the risk weight. For delta and vega, Kb is the square root of the weighted-sensitivity quadratic form. Across buckets use Sb = sum WS. Only if that outer radicand is negative, replace Sb with its value capped to [−Kb, Kb] and recalculate.'],['Special buckets matter','Other buckets do not all behave like ordinary diversified buckets. CSR bucket 16, ACTP bucket 16, non-ACTP bucket 25 and equity bucket 11 have special within-bucket treatment. Non-ACTP bucket 25 is also added without cross-bucket diversification. The named portfolios below avoid these exceptional buckets; the reference notes explain them.']],compute(s){const scenarios=['low','medium','high'],rows=[],totals=scenarios.map(sc=>{let total=0;classes.forEach(c=>{const base={a:s.scale,b:s.hedge*s.scale/100,c:.7*s.scale,d:.4*s.hedge*s.scale/100,vol:25,up:8,down:14};const charges=['delta','vega','curvature'].map(mode=>calc(c,{...base,mode},sc).capital*(mode==='delta'?1:s.option/100));total+=F.sum(charges);rows.push([sc,c.name,...charges.map(m),m(F.sum(charges))]);});return total});const win=totals.indexOf(Math.max(...totals));return {stats:[['SBM',m(totals[win])],['Binding scenario',scenarios[win]],['Risk classes','7']],bars:bars(scenarios,totals),headers:['Scenario','Risk class','Delta','Vega','Curvature','Subtotal'],rows,explanation:`The ${scenarios[win]} scenario gives the largest whole-portfolio charge, ${m(totals[win])}. Each total adds all seven risk classes and all three risk types before the maximum is chosen. Picking a different winning scenario for every line would overstate this calculation.`,note:'Each class can be explored in detail next. The table shows all 21 class/scenario combinations.'};}});
 classes.forEach(c=>L.push({id:c.id,name:c.name,stage:'03 · SBM risk-class labs',title:c.title,intro:c.intuition,chart:c.name+' · from risk factors to capital',inputs:[select('mode','Risk component',[['delta','Delta · small market moves'],['vega','Vega · implied volatility'],['curvature','Curvature · nonlinear repricing']],'delta'),slider('a','Bucket A · first exposure',-200,200,5,100),slider('b','Bucket A · second exposure',-200,200,5,-60),slider('c','Bucket B · first exposure',-200,200,5,70),slider('d','Bucket B · second exposure',-200,200,5,-25),{...slider('vol','Implied volatility',5,100,1,25,'%'),when:s=>s.mode==='vega'},{...slider('up','Up-shock residual loss / exposure',-30,30,1,8,'%'),when:s=>s.mode==='curvature'},{...slider('down','Down-shock residual loss / exposure',-30,30,1,14,'%'),when:s=>s.mode==='curvature'},select('scenario','Inspect scenario',[['low','Low correlation'],['medium','Medium correlation'],['high','High correlation']],'medium')],challenge:'Compare a hedge in the same bucket with a hedge in the other bucket. Then change the risk component.',formula:'WSk = RWk × sk\nKb = √max(0, Σ WSk² + Σk≠l ρkl WSk WSl)\nK = √(Σ Kb² + Σb≠c γbc Sb Sc)',key:'Correlations and weights are prescribed; exposures and repricing outputs are teaching inputs.',simpleTitle:'What gets grouped together?',simple:c.mapping,scope:c.profile+' Sliders are net sensitivities per unit rate/relative spot move in delta; unscaled dV/dσ in vega. Curvature starts from synthetic full-revaluation P&Ls built from the entered exposures and signed residual-loss percentages, not a real-instrument pricing model.',pages:c.pages.concat([[331,'325s · volatility-scaled vega'],[360,'325ax–ay · curvature / vega']]),notes:[['Why these correlations?',c.specific],['How to read the curvature view','CVR+ = −(up-shock P&L − RW × delta); CVR− = −(down-shock P&L + RW × delta). Positive CVR means loss beyond the delta estimate. Within each bucket take the larger of K+ and K−; ties use the larger CVR sum (otherwise down). Correlation terms vanish when both CVRs are negative. Use the chosen direction’s CVR sum across buckets.'],['Sensitivity units and model boundaries','A 1 bp rate/spread repricing change is divided by 0.0001; a 1% relative spot repricing change is divided by 0.01. Vega is the 1 percentage-point volatility repricing change divided by 0.01, then multiplied by current volatility. This lab accepts these net inputs; it does not derive them from trade cash flows.'],...(c.id==='csr'?[['Covered bonds and sovereign mapping','Bucket 8 has 1.5% RW for covered bonds rated AA− or better and 2.5% otherwise. The abbreviated reference labels require the rating and counterparty criteria in Article 325ah; the UK sovereign/Bank of England treatment in bucket 1 is specific to this instrument.']]:[])],reference:{headers:c.refHeaders||['Bucket / factor','Delta RW'],rows:c.rows},compute(s){const r=calc(c,s,s.scenario),all=['low','medium','high'].map(sc=>calc(c,s,sc).capital);const rows=[...r.rows];r.buckets.forEach((b,i)=>rows.push(s.mode==='curvature'?[c.buckets[i]+' · aggregate','K+ '+m(b.ku),'K− '+m(b.kd),'Use '+b.direction,'Kb '+m(b.k),'Sb '+m(b.s),'ρ '+pct(r.rho[i])]:[c.buckets[i]+' · aggregate','Sb '+m(r.ss[i]),'ρ '+pct(r.rho[i]),'Kb '+m(b.k)]));return {stats:[['Selected charge',m(r.capital)],['Cross-bucket γ',pct(r.gamma)],['Largest scenario',m(Math.max(...all))]],bars:bars(['Low','Medium','High'],all),headers:s.mode==='curvature'?['Factor','Delta input','Shock RW','Full up P&L','Full down P&L','CVR+','CVR−']:['Factor','Regulatory sensitivity','Risk weight','Weighted sensitivity'],rows,formula:s.mode==='curvature'?'CVR± = −(full shock P&L ∓ RW × delta)\nKb = max(Kb+, Kb−);  ψ(x,y) = 0 if both are negative, otherwise 1\nCurvature = √max(0, Σ Kb² + Σb≠c γbc Sb Sc ψ(Sb,Sc))':s.mode==='vega'?'sk = (dV / dσk) × σk;  WSk = RWk × sk\nKb = √max(0, Σ WSk² + Σk≠l ρkl WSk WSl)\nK = √(Σ Kb² + Σb≠c γbc Sb Sc)':undefined,key:`${s.mode==='curvature'?'Curvature uses squared delta correlations before scenario scaling.':s.mode==='vega'?'Vega correlations add the option-expiry decay; GIRR also uses underlying residual maturity.':'Net identical factors before weighting.'} Cross-bucket γ in this view: ${pct(r.gamma)}.`,explanation:`For ${c.name}, the ${s.scenario} ${s.mode} charge is ${m(r.capital)}. ${s.mode==='curvature'?'The table removes the linear delta contribution from both full-revaluation P&Ls, then chooses a direction separately for each bucket.':s.mode==='vega'?`The entered dV/dσ is multiplied by ${s.vol}% volatility before applying the vega weight.`:'Opposing signs can hedge, but different factors only receive the prescribed correlation benefit.'} ${r.fallback?'The outer radicand was negative, so the alternative capped-Sb rule is active.':''}`,note:'Scenario maxima here are for this one class/component. The preceding SBM lesson takes the maximum only after adding the entire portfolio.'};}}));
 L.push({id:'rrao',name:'RRAO · residual risks',stage:'04 · Default and residual risks',title:'The risks a sensitivity can leave behind',intro:'RRAO adds a gross-notional charge for exotic underlyings and other residual risks. A small delta does not imply a small add-on.',chart:'Notional, classification and exemptions',inputs:[slider('exotic','Exotic-underlying gross notional',0,1000,10,100),slider('other','Other residual-risk gross notional',0,1000,10,200),select('eligible','Other-risk listed / clearing eligibility',[['no','Neither exemption applies'],['yes','Listed or eligible for central clearing']],'no'),slider('matched','Perfectly matched qualifying offsets',0,100,5,0,'%')],challenge:'Turn on clearing eligibility. Why does the exotic-underlying charge survive?',formula:'RRAO = 1% × eligible exotic notional + 0.1% × eligible other-residual notional',key:'Gross notional after applicable exclusions. Use 1% for an instrument with an exotic underlying, without also charging the same notional at 0.1% here.',simpleTitle:'The payoff can hide risk between the lines',simple:'A barrier can switch a payoff on or off. A weather derivative can reference an underlying outside the ordinary market-risk factor set. RRAO recognises risks that the standard sensitivities do not adequately describe.',scope:'Two disjoint synthetic portfolios. The matched-offset slider assumes every removed portion meets the exact third-party matching conditions in Article 325u; it is not generic hedge netting.',pages:[[333,'325u · residual risk add-on']],notes:[['Classification examples','Exotic underlyings include longevity, weather, catastrophe and future realised volatility. Other residual risks can include path-dependent, multi-underlying or behavioural optionality, and ACTP positions subject to the specified hedge exception. A complex product is not automatically exotic.'],['Exemptions are conditional','Exchange listing or eligibility for central clearing removes the other-residual charge, not the exotic-underlying charge. Qualifying perfectly matching third-party offsets may be excluded; where notionals differ, residual notional remains. A special alternative for disproportionate charges needs instrument-specific PRA permission.'],['What does not automatically trigger RRAO?','Cheapest-to-deliver risk, volatility smile, eligible index correlation risk and certain dividend risks do not by themselves require this add-on under the exclusions in Article 325u. Check the payoff and prescribed conditions, not just a product nickname.']],compute(s){const ex=s.exotic*(1-s.matched/100),ot=s.other*(1-s.matched/100)*(s.eligible==='yes'?0:1),a=ex*.01,b=ot*.001;return {stats:[['RRAO',m(a+b)],['Exotic rate','1%'],['Other rate','0.1%']],bars:bars(['Exotic','Other residual'],[a,b]),headers:['Category','Chargeable notional','Rate','Capital'],rows:[['Exotic underlying',m(ex),'1%',m(a)],['Other residual',m(ot),'0.1%',m(b)],['Total','—','—',m(a+b)]],explanation:`RRAO is ${m(a+b)}. ${s.eligible==='yes'?'Listing/clearing eligibility removes the other-residual component, but the exotic charge remains.':'Both categories remain chargeable after the assumed qualifying offsets.'} A delta hedge alone does not change this gross-notional calculation.`,note:'The offset percentage represents exact qualifying matches, not ordinary economic hedges.'};}});
 L.push({id:'drc',name:'DRC · issuer default',stage:'04 · Default and residual risks',title:'What is lost when an issuer defaults?',intro:'Build jump-to-default exposure, apply maturity and netting rules, then calculate a bucket default-risk charge.',chart:'From default exposure to capital',inputs:[slider('face','Issuer A · bond face value',10,200,5,100),slider('price','Issuer A · price (% of par)',20,120,1,95,'%'),select('seniority','Bond seniority',[['senior','Senior debt · 75% LGD'],['sub','Non-senior debt · 100% LGD'],['covered','Covered bond · 25% LGD']],'senior'),slider('hedge','Eligible same-issuer short JTD',0,200,5,30),slider('other','Different-issuer short JTD',0,200,5,25),slider('maturity','Remaining maturity',.05,2,.05,1,'years'),select('rating','Issuer A rating',[['AAA','AAA'],['AA','AA'],['A','A'],['BBB','BBB'],['BB','BB'],['B','B'],['CCC','CCC and below'],['unrated','Unrated'],['default','Defaulted']],'BBB')],challenge:'Move a hedge from the same issuer to a different issuer. Why does its capital benefit change?',formula:'Long JTD = max(Vbefore − Vdefault, 0)\nWtS = Σ net long JTD / (Σ net long JTD + Σ |net short JTD|)\nDRCb = max(Σ RW × long − WtS × Σ RW × |short|, 0)',key:'Maturity scaling = min(1, max(0.25, maturity in years)). Sum DRC across corporate, sovereign and local/municipal buckets.',simpleTitle:'Spread risk and default risk are different events',simple:'A spread can widen while a borrower keeps paying. Default can destroy the remaining value in one jump. DRC measures the incremental loss from today’s marked value, not a loss already recognised in the price.',scope:'One corporate bucket. Same-issuer short exposure is assumed to satisfy seniority and netting rules; both it and the bond share the selected maturity. The different-issuer short is a one-year BBB exposure. LGDs are prescribed SA values, not forecasts.',pages:[[336,'325v–y · JTD and maturity'],[339,'325z · ratings, buckets and hedge benefit']],reference:{headers:['Rating','Default RW'],rows:[['AAA','0.5%'],['AA','2%'],['A','3%'],['BBB','6%'],['BB','15%'],['B','30%'],['CCC and below','50%'],['Unrated','15%'],['Defaulted','100%']]},notes:[['Seniority is not optional','Net same-obligor long and short only when the short has the same or lower seniority than the long, subject to Article 325w. Different obligors do not directly net. Their short exposure receives limited benefit through WtS.'],['Other instruments and exceptions','Equity defaults to zero. Derivatives require revaluation at default rather than mechanically applying bond LGD. Equity maturity choices and derivative contract maturity have specific rules. Exposures qualifying for a zero credit-risk weight under the cited standardised credit provisions receive a zero default-risk weight.']],compute(s){const lgd={senior:.75,sub:1,covered:.25}[s.seniority],rw={AAA:.005,AA:.02,A:.03,BBB:.06,BB:.15,B:.30,CCC:.50,unrated:.15,default:1}[s.rating],before=s.face*s.price/100,after=s.rating==='default'?before:s.face*(1-lgd),gross=Math.max(before-after,0),factor=F.maturity(s.maturity),net=s.rating==='default'?0:(gross-s.hedge)*factor,rr=F.drc(net>0?[{jtd:net,rw}]:[],[{jtd:s.other,rw:.06},...(net<0?[{jtd:-net,rw}]:[])]);return {stats:[['Bucket DRC',m(rr.capital)],['Hedge-benefit ratio',pct(rr.hbr)],['Maturity factor',n(factor)]],bars:bars(['Gross long JTD','Net long JTD','Weighted long','DRC'],[gross,Math.max(net,0),rr.wl,rr.capital]),headers:['Step','Amount / factor','Reason'],rows:[['Before default',m(before),'Face × current price'],['After default',m(after),s.rating==='default'?'Already reflected in current value':'Face × (1 − prescribed LGD)'],['Gross long JTD',m(gross),'Positive remaining loss'],['Net scaled issuer A JTD',m(net),s.rating==='default'?'No further issuer-A default jump':'(Gross long − eligible short) × maturity factor'],['Other short JTD',m(s.other),'Different BBB issuer, one year'],['WtS',pct(rr.hbr),'Unweighted net long / total absolute net JTD'],['Weighted long / short',m(rr.wl)+' / '+m(rr.ws),'Apply rating weights after netting'],['DRC',m(rr.capital),'Floor weighted long minus WtS × weighted short at zero']],explanation:`The bond loses ${m(gross)} from its current marked value on default. After the eligible same-issuer offset and maturity scaling, issuer A has ${m(net)} signed net JTD. The different-issuer short receives only the ${pct(rr.hbr)} hedge-benefit ratio.`,note:s.rating==='default'?'Issuer A is already defaulted and reflected in current value: its incremental JTD and same-issuer default hedge are zero. The different-issuer short remains.':'This portfolio contains a single corporate bucket; a complete SA DRC adds the separate bucket charges.'};}});
 L.push({id:'drc-sec',name:'DRC · securitisations',stage:'04 · Default and residual risks',title:'Default losses through a tranche',intro:'Securitisation default risk has its own netting, weighting and aggregation rules. Compare non-ACTP with correlation trading.',chart:'Two default-risk buckets',inputs:[select('kind','Portfolio',[['non','Non-ACTP · two securitisation buckets'],['actp','ACTP · two index buckets']],'non'),slider('longA','Bucket A · net long JTD',0,200,5,100),slider('shortA','Bucket A · net short JTD',0,200,5,50),slider('longB','Bucket B · net long JTD',0,200,5,40),slider('shortB','Bucket B · net short JTD',0,200,5,80),slider('rw','Banking-book securitisation RW',20,1250,10,250,'%')],challenge:'Keep the total long and short exposure unchanged, but move it between buckets. Does ACTP use a local or global hedge ratio?',formula:'Tranche DRC risk weight = 8% × banking-book securitisation risk weight\nDRCb = max(weighted long JTD − WtS × weighted short JTD, 0)',key:'The banking-book risk weight is supplied, not calculated here. A 250% banking-book RW becomes a 20% DRC weight.',simpleTitle:'A tranche is a loss allocation rule',simple:'Two tranches can share a pool but absorb losses at different points. Their default exposures do not automatically offset. Non-ACTP permits narrowly defined matching and replication; ACTP requires its own index and tranche treatment.',scope:'Inputs are already-netted, maturity-scaled JTDs. All positions are cash tranches, so weighted JTD is capped at fair value. The example omits the upstream securitisation hierarchy and instrument replication calculations.',pages:[[340,'325aa–ac · non-ACTP DRC'],[342,'325ad · ACTP DRC']],notes:[['Non-ACTP netting and buckets','Gross JTD is market/fair value. Offsetting normally requires the same pool and tranche; different pools cannot offset merely because attachment and detachment points match. There are 45 buckets: one corporate bucket plus eleven asset classes across four regions. DRC is summed without diversification across buckets.'],['ACTP is different','Hedges are assigned to ACTP rather than double counted under non-securitisation DRC. Index family, series and tranche restrictions apply. WtS is calculated across the entire ACTP. Tranche weights use 8% of the banking-book RW; non-tranched products use the non-securitisation rating weights.'],['Reading the supplied ACTP formula literally','Article 325ad(3) in this instrument floors DRCb at zero. Paragraph (4) then sums positive DRCb plus 50% of negative DRCb, with an outer zero floor. Because paragraph (3) has already floored each bucket, its negative-bucket term cannot activate in this illustration. This app follows the supplied text rather than silently substituting a different jurisdiction’s convention.']],compute(s){const rw=Math.min(s.rw/100*.08,1),long=s.longA+s.longB,short=s.shortA+s.shortB,global=long+short?long/(long+short):0,rows=[],charges=[[s.longA,s.shortA],[s.longB,s.shortB]].map(([l,sh],i)=>{const h=s.kind==='actp'?global:l+sh?l/(l+sh):0,raw=rw*l-h*rw*sh,k=Math.max(raw,0);rows.push([i?'B':'A',m(l),m(sh),pct(h),m(raw),m(k)]);return k});return {stats:[['DRC total',m(F.sum(charges))],['Tranche weight',pct(rw)],['WtS basis',s.kind==='actp'?'Whole ACTP':'Each bucket']],bars:bars(['Bucket A','Bucket B','Total'],[...charges,F.sum(charges)]),headers:['Bucket','Net long JTD','Net short JTD','WtS','Before floor','DRCb'],rows,explanation:`${s.kind==='actp'?'Both index buckets use the whole-ACTP':'Each non-ACTP bucket uses its own'} hedge-benefit ratio. The cash-tranche weight is ${pct(rw)}, and the bucket charges total ${m(F.sum(charges))}.`,note:'The detailed article notes below explain the source’s ACTP floor convention and the netting assumptions.'};}});
})();
